<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#indus-script">Glossary</a> · Term · script</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indus script</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2800–1900 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/29th-century-bce">29th century BCE</a> · Also known as Harappan script, Indus Valley script</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> script, also called the Harappan script, is the set of signs used by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>, found chiefly on stamp seals, seal impressions and pottery. Early examples date from about 2800–2600 BCE, and by 1992 some 4,000 inscribed objects bearing over 400 distinct signs were known, generally read from right to left. Because the inscriptions are very short, averaging about five signs, and no bilingual text has been found, it remains undeciphered, and scholars disagree whether it records a language at all.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/indus-script.jpg" alt="Indus script" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: World Imaging · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IndusValleySeals.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
